WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.870
So now we have a well defined
computational problem

00:00:02.870 --> 00:00:05.650
that's going to model what the mass
spectrometer is telling us.

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:10.840
And here we'll study a first idea of an
algorithm for it.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:14.580
First, I'll tell you that the mass of
the entire peptide is usually known.

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:18.710
So we'll usually already know that Tyrocidine B1 has mass 1322.

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:22.520
Our algorithm is going to then say,

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:25.550
all right, let's generate every possible
peptide of that mass

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:29.380
so in this case, for Tyrocidine
B1, it’s 1322.

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:33.150
Let's form the spectrum of every one of
those peptides,

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:37.060
and then let's look for matches with the
given spectrum among those peptides.

00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:40.460
This is an example of a brute force
algorithm.

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:42.430
In general, a brute force algorithm is
going

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:46.300
to try all possible solutions that satisfy
a certain condition.

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:53.060
So then our question is, to determine how
long is this going to take,

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:56.590
we ask, how many peptides have a given
integer mass?

00:00:56.590 --> 00:01:02.250
So in specific, how many peptides have
integer mass equal to 1322?

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:06.020
Here's a graph that shows the integer mass
of a peptide

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:10.330
versus the number of peptides
that have that mass.

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:15.070
And if we look at 1322, I've shown red
lines here, we go up from 1322

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:15.740
and then we go over,

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:18.140
and we see it's almost at 10 to the
14th.

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:22.090
So the answer is, TRILLIONS of different
peptides have this mass.

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:23.220
And that's not going to be good.

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:26.360
We don't want to have to consider
trillions of different possibilities.

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:31.080
So let's ask ourselves, then, what is it

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:34.040
that makes brute force so bad to begin
with?

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:35.880
Or, at least, this brute force algorithm?

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:39.080
Some brute force algorithms may work well,
if they choose a correct condition.

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:43.650
Here's a peptide, NQEL,

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:45.760
that we're going to start working
with,

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:47.320
snd another peptide, TMDH.

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:54.810
The total mass of NQEL is 484,
and the total mass of TMDH is 484.

00:01:54.810 --> 00:01:56.860
But they're completely different.

00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:59.340
They don't have any of the same amino
acids.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:03.400
By extension, they're not going to have any of the same
sub-peptides.

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:06.250
And so we need to find a way to exclude
this completely.

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:09.790
We don't want to ever have to encounter this
peptide.

00:02:09.790 --> 00:02:09.980
 

00:02:09.980 --> 00:02:14.240
The way that we're going to get at
that

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:17.520
is to say let's look at the spectra of these
two peptides.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:19.360
So on the left here I've shown the
spectrum of TMDH

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:22.250
and on the right I've shown the
spectrum of NQEL.

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:26.230
They completely disagree.

00:02:26.230 --> 00:02:27.450
So you may notice this.

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:29.220
They agree at 0, and they agree at 484.

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:33.750
And nowhere in between do they have the
same two of the same masses.

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:37.050
We want to use this to our advantage.

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:39.810
We want to use this to start designing an
algorithm that's going

00:02:39.810 --> 00:02:43.760
to eliminate TMDH based on its
spectrum, which is not good.

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:49.110
So our idea is: rather than try to form
big peptides all at once,

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:49.746
let's slowly

00:02:49.746 --> 00:02:53.370
build up our candidate solutions from
smaller peptides.

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:55.870
At each stage, we're going to test its
spectrum,

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:57.670
and make sure it's on the right
track.

00:02:57.670 --> 00:03:00.420
And if it's not on the right track, we're
going to kick it out.

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:02.770
That's going to allow us to restrict

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:04.930
the total number of peptides that are under
consideration.

